## What's wrong in your code

The service and the controller disagree on the exact error text:

| Where | Message |
|---|---|
| `updateVenueService` throws | `" not found"` (with a leading space) |
| `deleteVenuesService` throws | `"not found"` |
| Both controllers check | `"Venue not found"` |

`===` on strings needs an exact match, so neither check ever passes. A missing venue falls through to the generic 500.

## The root cause: matching on message text

The quickest fix is to make the strings equal. But that bug will come back, because a message is text written for humans. Someone rewords it, fixes a typo, or translates it, and the controller silently stops matching. The compiler can't warn you, because it's just a string.

## Options

| Option | Tradeoff |
|---|---|
| Make the strings match | 10 seconds, but the same fragility stays. |
| Custom error class (`NotFoundError`) and `instanceof` | Type-safe and the standard in larger codebases. Needs a small class hierarchy. |
| **Error with a `statusCode`, using your `createError` helper** | You already use it in the hold service, so it's consistent. The controller reads a number, not text. |
| Error codes (`error.code = "VENUE_NOT_FOUND"`) | Good for APIs where clients branch on the code. More ceremony than you need now. |

Production systems usually do the class or code approach plus **one central error-handling middleware**, so controllers don't each repeat the mapping. For now, the `statusCode` approach teaches the same idea with the least change.

## The fix: services

Import `createError` the same way as in the hold service, then:

```js
if (result.rowCount === 0) {
  throw createError("Venue not found", 404);
}
```

Use that same line in both `updateVenueService` and `deleteVenuesService`.

## The fix: controllers

Validate the id first, then map errors by `statusCode`. This is the update controller:

```js
export const updateVenue = async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const id = Number(req.params.id);
    if (!Number.isInteger(id) || id <= 0) {
      return res.status(400).json({ success: false, message: "Invalid venue id" });
    }

    const venue = await updateVenueService(req.body, id);

    return res.status(200).json({
      success: true,
      message: "Venue updated successfully",
      data: venue,
    });
  } catch (error) {
    if (error.statusCode) {
      return res.status(error.statusCode).json({ success: false, message: error.message });
    }
    console.error("Update venue error:", error);
    return res.status(500).json({ success: false, message: "Failed to update venue" });
  }
};
```

The delete controller is the same, with one addition inside `catch`, before the `statusCode` check:

```js
if (error.code === "23503") {
  return res.status(409).json({
    success: false,
    message: "Venue has events and cannot be deleted",
  });
}
```

Why that extra case: your `events` table references `venues` through a foreign key. Deleting a venue that has events makes Postgres throw `23503`, which would be a 500. It isn't a server failure, it's a conflict with existing data, so it's a **409**.

## Why `statusCode` is safe here

You only forward `error.message` to the client when the error came from `createError`, because only those have `statusCode`. Raw Postgres errors have `code` but no `statusCode`, so they go to the generic 500 branch and their internal details (table names, constraint names) never leak to the client.

## One thing to watch in update

`coalesce($1, name)` means "keep the old value if the new one is null". That works for partial updates, but you can never intentionally set a field to `NULL`. For venues that's fine, since all four fields are required. Also, an empty body `{}` still runs the update and returns the venue unchanged. That's acceptable, and you can reject it with a 400 later if you want.

